\title{Tropical Cryptocurrency: Exact Fibers, Efficient Inversion,\\Universal Two-Key Collisions, and Concavity}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We analyze a tropical analogue of cryptographic hashing built from min-plus linear forms. For a nonempty real message vector $m\in\R^k$ and key $h\in\R^k$, the scalar map is $T_h(m)=\min_i(m_i+h_i)$, while a two-key variant returns $(T_h(m),T_{h'}(m))$. We characterize every scalar fiber exactly: the output equals $y$ precisely when all coordinate sums lie above $y$ and at least one attains $y$. This yields the canonical preimage $m_i=y-h_i$, proving surjectivity and linear-time inversion. Increasing a coordinate that is distinct from a chosen minimizer leaves the output unchanged. Applying this principle to two keys proves that, for every $k\ge3$, every message and every pair of keys admits a distinct message with exactly the same two-key output; indeed, every message lies on an unbounded collision ray. Finally, we prove concavity of the two-coordinate minimum along line segments. These results separate useful tropical optimization geometry from cryptographic one-wayness and show that future designs require constraints, nonlinear mixing, or other genuine coordinate coupling.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The min-plus semiring replaces ordinary addition and multiplication by
\[
a\oplus b=\min(a,b),\qquad a\otimes b=a+b.
\]
It naturally describes shortest paths, scheduling, logistics, and dynamic programming: serial costs add, while competing alternatives are minimized. This optimization interpretation motivates the proposal that cryptocurrency mining might be based on tropical mathematics rather than conventional bit mixing.

For a message $m=(m_1,\ldots,m_k)$ and key $h=(h_1,\ldots,h_k)$, the simplest proposal is the tropical inner product
\[
T_h(m)=\bigoplus_{i=1}^k(m_i\otimes h_i)=\min_{1\le i\le k}(m_i+h_i).
\]
Evaluation requires one pass through the coordinates. One might nevertheless suspect that inversion requires a combinatorial search for an active coordinate, or that two independently keyed outputs suppress collisions. We show that neither is true for unrestricted real messages.

Our contributions are an exact fiber theorem, an explicit inverse construction for every target, a stability theorem for inactive coordinates, a universal two-key collision theorem in dimensions at least three, and a concavity theorem connecting the construction to tropical optimization geometry. The conclusions are constructive and deterministic. They do not rely on random-key models or asymptotic collision estimates.

\section{Definitions and computational model}

Let $k\ge1$. A \emph{message} is $m\in\R^k$ and a \emph{key} is $h\in\R^k$.

\begin{definition}[Scalar min-plus hash]
For a key $h$ and message $m$, define
\[
T_h(m)=\min_{1\le i\le k}(m_i+h_i).
\]
A coordinate $p$ is \emph{active} if $m_p+h_p=T_h(m)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Two-key min-plus hash]
For keys $h,h'\in\R^k$, define
\[
T_{h,h'}^{(2)}(m)=\bigl(T_h(m),T_{h'}(m)\bigr)\in\R^2.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fiber and collision]
For $y\in\R$, the scalar fiber is
\[
F_h(y)=\{m\in\R^k:T_h(m)=y\}.
\]
A collision is a pair $m\ne m'$ with equal outputs under the map in question.
\end{definition}

In the unit-cost real-arithmetic model, scalar evaluation forms $k$ sums and maintains a running minimum, using $O(k)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space. Two-key evaluation maintains two running minima and has the same asymptotic complexity.

\section{Exact fibers and unrestricted inversion}

\begin{lemma}[Coordinate upper bound]
For every $h,m\in\R^k$ and every coordinate $i$,
\[
T_h(m)\le m_i+h_i.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The left side is the minimum of a finite family containing $m_i+h_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Attainment]
For every $h,m\in\R^k$, there is a coordinate $p$ satisfying
\[
m_p+h_p=T_h(m).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A nonempty finite set of real numbers contains an element equal to its minimum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact Fiber Theorem]
Let $h,m\in\R^k$, where $k\ge1$, and let $y\in\R$. Then $T_h(m)=y$ if and only if
\[
y\le m_i+h_i\quad\text{for every }i
\]
and
\[
m_p+h_p=y\quad\text{for at least one coordinate }p.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $T_h(m)=y$, the coordinate upper bound gives every inequality and attainment gives the equality. Conversely, if every coordinate sum is at least $y$, their minimum is at least $y$. If one coordinate sum equals $y$, the minimum is at most $y$. Therefore it equals $y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Geometric fiber description]
The fiber over $y$ is
\[
F_h(y)=\bigcup_{p=1}^k
\{m\in\R^k:m_p=y-h_p,\ m_i\ge y-h_i\text{ for every }i\}.
\]
Thus it is a union of closed polyhedral faces of the translated orthant
\[
\prod_{i=1}^k[y-h_i,\infty).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Subtract $h_i$ from the inequalities and active equality in the Exact Fiber Theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Canonical Preimage Theorem]
For any key $h\in\R^k$ and target $y\in\R$, define $m^\star$ by
\[
m_i^\star=y-h_i\qquad(1\le i\le k).
\]
Then $T_h(m^\star)=y$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every coordinate sum satisfies $m_i^\star+h_i=y$, so their minimum is $y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Surjectivity and linear-time inversion]
For every key $h$, the map $T_h:\R^k\to\R$ is surjective. A preimage of any target can be constructed in $O(k)$ time.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The canonical construction supplies a preimage for every $y$ and uses one subtraction per coordinate.
\end{proof}

Hence unrestricted inversion does not require enumeration of tropical paths. Its asymptotic cost matches forward evaluation.

\section{Stability and scalar collision rays}

\begin{lemma}[Inactive-Coordinate Update Principle]
Suppose $p$ is active for $(h,m)$, let $q\ne p$, and let $d\ge0$. Define $m'$ by $m'_q=m_q+d$ and $m'_i=m_i$ for $i\ne q$. Then
\[
T_h(m')=T_h(m).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The active value at $p$ is unchanged, so the new minimum is at most the old one. Every unchanged coordinate remains at least the old minimum, while the modified coordinate has only increased. Thus the new minimum is also at least the old one.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Scalar collision rays]
If $k\ge2$, every message has a distinct scalar collision. More strongly, after choosing active $p$ and any $q\ne p$, the ray
\[
\{m+d e_q:d\ge0\}
\]
lies in one scalar fiber.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply the update principle for every $d\ge0$; any $d>0$ changes the message.
\end{proof}

The minimum records an active value but forgets the margins of inactive coordinates. This loss of information produces continua of collisions rather than isolated pairs.

\section{Universal two-key collisions}

\begin{lemma}[Avoiding two witnesses]
If $k\ge3$ and $p,r\in\{1,\ldots,k\}$, there exists $q$ with $q\ne p$ and $q\ne r$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
At most two indices are forbidden and at least three are available.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universal Two-Key Collision Theorem]
Let $k\ge3$. For every $h,h',m\in\R^k$, there exists $m'\ne m$ such that
\[
T_{h,h'}^{(2)}(m')=T_{h,h'}^{(2)}(m).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $p$ active for $(h,m)$ and $r$ active for $(h',m)$. Choose $q$ distinct from both and set $m'=m+e_q$. The update principle applied using witness $p$ preserves $T_h$, while the same principle using witness $r$ preserves $T_{h'}$. Since the $q$th coordinate increased, $m'\ne m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Unbounded two-key collision ray]
Under the same hypotheses, some coordinate $q$ satisfies
\[
T_{h,h'}^{(2)}(m+d e_q)=T_{h,h'}^{(2)}(m)
\]
for every $d\ge0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Use the coordinate avoiding both witnesses and apply the update principle to both keys for arbitrary $d\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Linear-time collision construction]
A two-key collision in dimension $k\ge3$ can be constructed in $O(k)$ time and $O(k)$ output space.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Scan once to locate a minimizer for each key, choose an index outside the witness set, copy the message, and increase that coordinate.
\end{proof}

The theorem is stronger than a probabilistic weakness: it applies to every key pair and every starting message. The condition $k\ge3$ is sufficient for this universal construction. It does not assert injectivity below that dimension; ties or shared witnesses can still produce lower-dimensional collisions.

\section{Concavity and tropical geometry}

\begin{theorem}[Pairwise Concavity Theorem]
For $v,w\in\R^2$ and $t\in[0,1]$,
\[
\min\bigl((1-t)v_0+tw_0,(1-t)v_1+tw_1\bigr)
\ge (1-t)\min(v_0,v_1)+t\min(w_0,w_1).
\]
Thus $(x_0,x_1)\mapsto\min(x_0,x_1)$ is concave.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $a=\min(v_0,v_1)$ and $b=\min(w_0,w_1)$. For each $i$, $v_i\ge a$ and $w_i\ge b$. Since $t$ and $1-t$ are nonnegative,
\[
(1-t)v_i+tw_i\ge(1-t)a+tb.
\]
Both interpolated coordinates obey this bound, and therefore so does their minimum.
\end{proof}

Concavity locates the construction within piecewise-linear optimization. It does not provide diffusion: broad fibers and recession directions coexist with the regularity of a concave graph.

\section{Algorithms and numerical interpretation}

\subsection{Scalar evaluation and certificate}
Scan the values $m_i+h_i$, retaining the smallest observed value and its index. The result and witness certify the output in $O(k)$ time. A full fiber certificate for a claimed $y$ checks all inequalities $m_i+h_i\ge y$ and checks that at least one equality occurs, also in $O(k)$ time.

\subsection{Canonical inversion}
Given $(h,y)$, output $(y-h_1,\ldots,y-h_k)$. This point lies at the intersection of all principal fiber faces because every coordinate is active. No combinatorial search is involved.

\subsection{Deterministic two-key collision algorithm}
Given $(h,h',m)$ with $k\ge3$, find indices $p$ and $r$ minimizing the two keyed coordinate-sum families. Choose $q\notin\{p,r\}$, choose $d>0$, and output $m+d e_q$. Finding the witnesses and copying the output take $O(k)$ time.

Numerical experiments can illustrate these identities, preferably with integer data to avoid floating-point ambiguity. Such experiments are not needed to estimate collision probability because the collision statement is deterministic. Timings against conventional cryptographic digests compare execution costs only; the domains, codomains, and security properties are fundamentally different.

\section{Cryptographic consequences and scope}

A cryptographic hash should resist preimage and collision attacks under a specified finite encoding and adversarial model. The scalar map fails unrestricted preimage resistance because every target has an explicit preimage. The two-key map fails unrestricted collision resistance because every message in dimension at least three lies on a collision ray.

The source of failure is lack of coordinate coupling. Each output component needs only one active witness. A fixed collection of uncoupled minima can leave coordinates that influence no selected witness, and increasing such coordinates is invisible.

The scope is important. Messages here range over all of $\R^k$; bounded alphabets could block an upward move at a boundary. Coordinates are independently adjustable; legal nonce formats or difference constraints may prohibit the constructed update. The circuit has one uncoupled min-plus linear layer; deeper nonlinear circuits may reuse and mix coordinates. Accordingly, the results do not establish complexity bounds for constrained variants. They identify exactly where additional hardness would need to enter.

\section{Applications beyond cryptography}

The Exact Fiber Theorem applies to any system reporting the earliest of several offset events. In scheduling, it classifies every schedule with the same earliest completion. In sensitivity analysis, the update principle identifies perturbations that preserve an optimum. In inverse optimization, the canonical preimage realizes a desired optimum directly. Geometrically, active sets index the polyhedral faces of each level set.

The two-key theorem is also an observability statement: two minimum sensors cannot distinguish every state of a system with at least three freely adjustable coordinates. Preserving one witness per sensor produces an unobservable direction. Concavity remains useful for optimization even though it is unfavorable for cryptographic sensitivity.

\section{Future work}

A first direction is constrained inversion under sparse integer difference constraints. The exact fiber inequalities could interact with the constraint graph in ways that yield dynamic programming on bounded-treewidth instances and hardness on unrestricted graphs.

A second direction is the general $r$-key case. The witness argument suggests that $r$ uncoupled min-plus forms on $k>r$ coordinates admit an unbounded collision ray through every message. A systematic theorem would quantify the obstruction for any fixed number of independent linear keys.

A third direction is generic fiber geometry. Simultaneous outputs form intersections of finite polyhedral unions. Active-set analysis should describe maximal dimensions and recession cones, with expected dimension at least $k-r$ under suitable genericity assumptions.

A fourth direction is nonlinear tropical circuitry over discrete alphabets. Coordinate mixing and repeated reuse may eliminate simple inactive-coordinate updates. Any security statement would require a precise encoding, adversarial model, and average-case assumption.

Finally, mining thresholds such as $T_h(m)<\tau$ become meaningful only when $m$ is generated by a constrained nonce map or feasible set. Recession-cone geometry may determine whether threshold satisfaction is automatic, impossible, or computationally nontrivial.

\section{Conclusion}

The scalar min-plus hash has explicit fibers and a canonical preimage for every target, making unrestricted inversion linear-time. Raising a non-witness coordinate preserves the output. With two keys and at least three coordinates, one can preserve one witness per component and raise a third coordinate, producing a deterministic collision and an unbounded collision ray through every message. Meanwhile, the two-coordinate minimum is concave, confirming the construction's natural optimization geometry. Algebraic elegance and computational usefulness do not imply cryptographic one-wayness: credible tropical mining must obtain difficulty from constraints, diffusion, and nonlinear coordinate coupling rather than from coordinatewise minima alone.

\end{document}

